 I checked the problem with documentation, FAQ, open issues, forum.opencv.org, Stack Overflow, etc and have not found any solution
 I updated to the latest OpenCV version and the issue is still there
 There is reproducer code and related data files (videos, images, onnx, etc)

System Information

OpenCV version: 4.12.0 (runtime reproduction); current 4.x at 2b6ea5f344c023825608987a2804997c051a6f51 and tag 5.0.0 (source inspection)
Operating System / Platform: Windows 11 x64, build 26200
Compiler: MSVC 19.50.35720
VideoCapture backend: MSMF

Detailed description
SourceReaderCB creates an unnamed auto-reset Event in its constructor:
SourceReaderCB() :
    m_nRefCount(0), m_hEvent(CreateEvent(NULL, FALSE, FALSE, NULL)), ...
The destructor does not close that owned handle:
virtual ~SourceReaderCB()
{
    CV_LOG_INFO(NULL, "terminating async callback");
}
As a result, repeatedly opening and releasing a camera with CAP_MSMF leaves approximately one additional Event handle per lifecycle after warm-up. This is a kernel handle leak rather than only allocator retention.
The same missing CloseHandle is present in OpenCV 4.12.0, 4.13.0, the current 4.x branch, and tag 5.0.0.
A build-matched A/B test was performed with OpenCV 4.12.0. The compiler, CMake options, camera, workload, executable, and source were identical; the only change was closing m_hEvent in the destructor.



Build
Event handles, cycle 4 -> 10
Total process handles, cycle 4 -> 10




Unpatched
+7
+20


With CloseHandle(m_hEvent)
0
+12



The patched build also completed 100/100 MSMF camera open/release lifecycles. Across cycles 51-100, the Event-handle band was 1 and the total process-handle band was 2, with no linear Event-handle growth.
Related reports: #19737, #13255, and https://github.com/markany-inc/aios-windows-agent/issues/24.
Steps to reproduce
Run repeated MSMF camera lifecycles and observe the process handles by object type, for example with Process Explorer or NtQuerySystemInformation(SystemExtendedHandleInformation):
#include <opencv2/opencv.hpp>

#include <chrono>
#include <thread>

int main()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; ++i)
    {
        cv::VideoCapture cap;
        if (!cap.open(0, cv::CAP_MSMF))
            return 1;

        cv::Mat frame;
        cap.read(frame);
        cap.release();
        std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(500));
    }
    return 0;
}
After the MSMF warm-up phase, the unpatched build accumulates Event handles as SourceReaderCB instances are destroyed. Closing the owned Event in SourceReaderCB::~SourceReaderCB() removes that linear growth.
