System Information
OpenCV version:    4.13.0 (with PR #28562 applied) and 5.0.0 (also current 5.x, 172ea5a00c)
IPP version:       2022.2.0 (ippicv bundled with 4.13.0) and 2026.0.0 (bundled with 5.x)
Operating System:  Ubuntu 24.04.4 LTS, x86_64
Compiler:          g++ 13.3.0
CMake:             4.2.0, default configuration with WITH_IPP=ON (the default)

Detailed description
(AI disclosure: Claude Fable-5 was used to diagnose and write up this issue after a manual library upgrade in a larger project caused unit tests to start failing.)
hal/ipp/src/warp_ipp.cpp routes cv::warpAffine with INTER_NEAREST to ipp::iwiWarpAffine for CV_16S (C1/C3/C4), CV_64F (C1/C3/C4) and CV_16UC4, per its impl[] dispatch table. The table's comment states:
#else // IPP_CALLS_ENFORCED is not defined, results are strictly aligned to OpenCV implementation
This is not the case: IPP rounds source coordinates differently from the native fixed-point implementation (warpAffineBlocklineNN, 10-bit AB_BITS arithmetic). For transforms with rotation and/or fractional translation, destination pixels whose source coordinates land near a half-pixel boundary resolve to a different source pixel than the native path. With INTER_NEAREST there is no blending, so the affected pixels take entirely different values.
Concretely: warping the same pixel values with the same transform gives different results for a CV_16S Mat (IPP path) than for a CV_16U Mat (native path) — about 0.05–0.07 % of pixels under a generic rotation.
This matters when CV_16S and CV_16U Mats are warped with the same transform and the outputs need to stay pixel-aligned. It also means results silently differ between x86_64 (IPP) and ARM (no IPP) builds.
Historical note: this appears to be the same behavior for which IPP warping was disabled from ~2017 through 4.11 (core/private.hpp):
#define IPP_DISABLE_WARPAFFINE          1 // Different results
The new IPP HAL re-enabled these dispatch entries without the underlying "different results" problem having been fixed; results are unchanged between IPP 2022.2.0 and 2026.0.0. (The 4.13.0 release additionally applied the transform inverted — that part is already fixed by #28554 / #28562. This report is about the residual mismatch that remains after that fix.)
Workarounds we found:

Build with WITH_IPP=OFF
Zero the warpAffine impl[] dispatch entries in warp_ipp.cpp (our current workaround)
cv::ipp::setUseIPP(false) at runtime — works on 4.x since 6bb3c31,
but not on 5.x, where warp_ipp.cpp is missing the
CV_HAL_CHECK_USE_IPP() check (reported separately as #29280 ).

Suggested fix: either make the IPP NEAREST coordinate rounding match the native fixed-point arithmetic, or remove the CV_16S/CV_64F/CV_16UC4 NEAREST entries from the non-enforced dispatch table so the "strictly aligned" contract holds (this would restore the pre-4.12 status quo, where IPP warpAffine was disabled with the comment "Different results").
Steps to reproduce
// g++ -O2 repro.cpp -o repro $(pkg-config --cflags --libs opencv4)  # or opencv5
// Needs only core + imgproc. Builds the affine matrix by hand (same formula as
// cv::getRotationMatrix2D) so it compiles on both 4.x and 5.x -- in 5.0,
// getRotationMatrix2D moved from imgproc to the new geometry module.
#include <opencv2/core.hpp>
#include <opencv2/core/utility.hpp>
#include <opencv2/imgproc.hpp>
#include <cmath>
#include <cstdio>

// Same convention as cv::getRotationMatrix2D(center, angle_deg, 1.0),
// plus an extra fractional translation (tx, ty).
static cv::Mat makeAffine(double angle_deg, double cx, double cy, double tx, double ty)
{
    double a = angle_deg * CV_PI / 180.0, c = std::cos(a), s = std::sin(a);
    cv::Mat M(2, 3, CV_64F);
    M.at<double>(0, 0) = c;  M.at<double>(0, 1) = s;  M.at<double>(0, 2) = (1 - c) * cx - s * cy + tx;
    M.at<double>(1, 0) = -s; M.at<double>(1, 1) = c;  M.at<double>(1, 2) = s * cx + (1 - c) * cy + ty;
    return M;
}

int main()
{
    printf("OpenCV %s\n", CV_VERSION);

    // Gradient image: any sampling difference changes pixel values.
    cv::Mat src16u(600, 800, CV_16UC1);
    for (int y = 0; y < src16u.rows; y++)
        for (int x = 0; x < src16u.cols; x++)
            src16u.at<ushort>(y, x) = (ushort)(1 + ((x * 7 + y * 13) % 30000));
    cv::Mat src16s;
    src16u.convertTo(src16s, CV_16S);  // identical values, valid int16 range

    int total_diff = 0;
    for (double angle : {0.0, 13.7, 45.0, 90.0})
    {
        cv::Mat M = makeAffine(angle, 400, 300, 37.3, -12.8);
        cv::Mat dst16u, dst16s, dst16s_as_u;
        cv::warpAffine(src16u, dst16u, M, cv::Size(900, 900), cv::INTER_NEAREST);
        cv::warpAffine(src16s, dst16s, M, cv::Size(900, 900), cv::INTER_NEAREST);
        dst16s.convertTo(dst16s_as_u, CV_16U);

        int diff = cv::countNonZero(dst16u != dst16s_as_u);
        total_diff += diff;
        printf("angle %5.1f: %6d of %d pixels differ (%.3f%%)\n",
               angle, diff, 900 * 900, 100.0 * diff / (900.0 * 900.0));
    }
    printf(total_diff == 0 ? "OK: 16U and 16S warps are identical\n"
                           : "BUG: 16U and 16S warps differ\n");
    return total_diff != 0;
}
Expected: INTER_NEAREST performs no value arithmetic, so warping identical pixel values with an identical transform should give bit-identical results regardless of Mat depth — and regardless of whether IPP or the native path executes.
Observed (x86_64, WITH_IPP=ON default build):
OpenCV 4.13.0 (+ PR #28562), IPP 2022.2.0:
angle   0.0:      0 of 810000 pixels differ (0.000%)
angle  13.7:    442 of 810000 pixels differ (0.055%)
angle  45.0:    534 of 810000 pixels differ (0.066%)
angle  90.0:      0 of 810000 pixels differ (0.000%)

OpenCV 5.x 172ea5a00c (identical warp code to 5.0.0), IPP 2026.0.0:
angle   0.0:      0 of 810000 pixels differ (0.000%)
angle  13.7:    454 of 810000 pixels differ (0.056%)
angle  45.0:    534 of 810000 pixels differ (0.066%)
angle  90.0:      0 of 810000 pixels differ (0.000%)

With WITH_IPP=OFF, or with the 16S rows of the impl[] table zeroed, the program reports 0 differing pixels at every angle.
