Detailed description
Summary
A signed integer left-shift of negative value occurs in the BRISK feature detector's subpixel refinement function when computing quadratic surface coefficients from AGAST corner scores.
Version
$ git describe
4.13.0-150-gc7732e1043

Description
The BRISK (Binary Robust Invariant Scalable Keypoints) feature detector contains an undefined behavior vulnerability in the BriskScaleSpace::subpixel2D function. This function performs subpixel-accurate localization of keypoints by fitting a 2D quadratic surface to a 3x3 neighborhood of AGAST corner scores. During coefficient calculation, it left-shifts the result of arithmetic operations that can produce negative values, which violates the C++11 standard.
The vulnerable code is in modules/features2d/src/brisk.cpp:
inline float
BriskScaleSpace::subpixel2D(const int s_0_0, const int s_0_1, const int s_0_2, 
                            const int s_1_0, const int s_1_1, const int s_1_2, 
                            const int s_2_0, const int s_2_1, const int s_2_2, 
                            float& delta_x, float& delta_y) const
{
  // the coefficients of the 2d quadratic function least-squares fit:
  int tmp1 = s_0_0 + s_0_2 - 2 * s_1_1 + s_2_0 + s_2_2;
  int coeff1 = 3 * (tmp1 + s_0_1 - ((s_1_0 + s_1_2) << 1) + s_2_1);
  int coeff2 = 3 * (tmp1 - ((s_0_1 + s_2_1) << 1) + s_1_0 + s_1_2);
  int tmp2 = s_0_2 - s_2_0;
  int tmp3 = (s_0_0 + tmp2 - s_2_2);
  int tmp4 = tmp3 - 2 * tmp2;
  int coeff3 = -3 * (tmp3 + s_0_1 - s_2_1);
  int coeff4 = -3 * (tmp4 + s_1_0 - s_1_2);
  int coeff5 = (s_0_0 - s_0_2 - s_2_0 + s_2_2) << 2;  // <--- Line 2037: UB when negative
  int coeff6 = -(s_0_0 + s_0_2 - ((s_1_0 + s_0_1 + s_1_2 + s_2_1) << 1) 
                 - 5 * s_1_1 + s_2_0 + s_2_2) << 1;  // <--- Line 2038: UB when negative
  // ... rest of function
}
The undefined behavior occurs when:

The expression (s_0_0 - s_0_2 - s_2_0 + s_2_2) evaluates to a negative value (e.g., -3)
Left-shifting a negative integer by any amount is undefined behavior in C++11 and later
AGAST corner scores range from 0-255, so their differences can legitimately be negative

This vulnerability is triggered during normal BRISK feature detection when:

An image is processed with BRISK::detectAndCompute()
The AGAST detector finds candidate keypoints in the image
The subpixel refinement process encounters a 3x3 score neighborhood where the diagonal differences produce a negative value
No special input is required - this can occur with regular images

Root Cause: The code uses left-shift operations (<< 2 and << 1) as optimization for multiplication by 4 and 2. While this works for non-negative values, it causes undefined behavior when the expression result is negative. The C++11 standard explicitly prohibits left-shifting negative integers ([expr.shift]/2).
Impact: This is a code quality issue rather than an exploitable security vulnerability. The undefined behavior:

Does not cause memory corruption or information leakage
Only affects the computed subpixel offset values (delta_x, delta_y)
May cause slightly inaccurate keypoint positions (typically sub-pixel deviations)
Will trigger UBSan errors when compiled with -fsanitize=undefined
In production builds, may produce inconsistent results across different compilers/optimization levels

Steps to reproduce
PoC Code
#include <cstddef>
#include <cstdint>
#include <vector>
#include <opencv2/core.hpp>
#include <opencv2/imgproc.hpp>
#include <opencv2/features2d.hpp>
#include <fuzzer/FuzzedDataProvider.h>

extern "C" int LLVMFuzzerTestOneInput(const uint8_t* data, size_t size) {
  if (size < 50) return 0;  // Minimum size for meaningful testing

  FuzzedDataProvider fdp(data, size);

  try {
    // Create a simple synthetic image for testing
    int width = fdp.ConsumeIntegralInRange<int>(10, 100);
    int height = fdp.ConsumeIntegralInRange<int>(10, 100);
    int channels = fdp.ConsumeBool() ? 1 : 3;
    int cv_type = (channels == 1) ? CV_8UC1 : CV_8UC3;

    cv::Mat image(height, width, cv_type);

    // Fill image with random data
    size_t bytes_needed = image.total() * image.elemSize();
    if (fdp.remaining_bytes() < bytes_needed) {
      return 0;
    }
    std::vector<uint8_t> image_data = fdp.ConsumeBytes<uint8_t>(bytes_needed);
    memcpy(image.data, image_data.data(), bytes_needed);

    // Simple morphology operation if single channel
    if (image.channels() == 1 && fdp.ConsumeBool()) {
      cv::Mat morphed;
      int kernel_size = fdp.ConsumeIntegralInRange<int>(1, 5);
      if (kernel_size % 2 == 0) kernel_size++;
      cv::Mat kernel = cv::getStructuringElement(
        cv::MORPH_RECT, 
        cv::Size(kernel_size, kernel_size)
      );
      int op_type = fdp.ConsumeIntegralInRange<int>(0, 2);
      cv::MorphTypes morph_op;
      switch (op_type) {
        case 0: morph_op = cv::MORPH_ERODE; break;
        case 1: morph_op = cv::MORPH_DILATE; break;
        case 2: morph_op = cv::MORPH_OPEN; break;
        default: morph_op = cv::MORPH_ERODE; break;
      }
      cv::morphologyEx(image, morphed, morph_op, kernel);
    }

    // BRISK feature detection
    int thresh = fdp.ConsumeIntegralInRange<int>(5, 50);
    int octaves = fdp.ConsumeIntegralInRange<int>(0, 3);
    float patternScale = fdp.ConsumeFloatingPointInRange<float>(0.5f, 1.5f);

    cv::Ptr<cv::BRISK> brisk_detector = cv::BRISK::create(thresh, octaves, patternScale);
    if (!brisk_detector) return 0;

    std::vector<cv::KeyPoint> keypoints;
    cv::Mat descriptors;

    brisk_detector->detectAndCompute(image, cv::noArray(), keypoints, descriptors);

  } catch (cv::Exception& e) {
    // Ignore OpenCV exceptions during fuzzing
  } catch (...) {
    // Catch any other exceptions
  }

  return 0;
}
