Detailed description
Summary
A signed integer overflow occurs in cv::Point_<int>::dot() when the intermediate multiplication x*pt.x overflows a 32-bit signed integer before saturate_cast can protect it. This is a library implementation bug, not a user error, as the function explicitly supports int-typed points and the same file's cross() and ddot() methods correctly guard against the identical hazard via type promotion.
Version
4.13.0-222-g5cbfe53798

Description
The vulnerable code is in modules/core/include/opencv2/core/types.hpp, line 1243–1246:
template<typename _Tp> inline
_Tp Point_<_Tp>::dot(const Point_& pt) const
{
    return saturate_cast<_Tp>(x*pt.x + y*pt.y);  // line 1245 — overflow here
}
When _Tp = int, both x*pt.x and y*pt.y are evaluated as 32-bit signed integer multiplications before saturate_cast is applied. For large-magnitude integer coordinates the product easily exceeds INT_MAX (2,147,483,647), which is undefined behavior (UB) in C++. saturate_cast operates on the already-corrupted result and provides no protection.
The root cause is the inconsistency with ddot() and cross() in the same file, which both promote operands to double before multiplying:
// line 1249 — ddot: correct, promotes first
double Point_<_Tp>::ddot(const Point_& pt) const {
    return (double)x*(double)(pt.x) + (double)y*(double)(pt.y);
}

// line 1255 — cross: correct, promotes first
double Point_<_Tp>::cross(const Point_& pt) const {
    return (double)x*pt.y - (double)y*pt.x;
}
Additionally, the library itself calls dot() on int points internally (line 1447), confirming the interface is fully intended to support Point_<int>:
// line 1447 — internal library usage on int points
template<> inline int normL2Sqr<int>(const Point_<int>& pt) { return pt.dot(pt); }
This internal call is equally vulnerable to any Point<int> whose coordinate magnitude exceeds ~46,341 (sqrt(INT_MAX)).
