Detailed description
This issue is with the AVFoundation backend specifically.
I usually use FFMPEG backend on my video caps when I want to do frame seeking, but there are files for which AVFoundation has much faster decoding speeds (ProRes).
The issue is pretty straight forward, VideoCapture::set(CAP_PROP_POS_FRAMES, N) drifts on any video which has a non-integer frame rate (23.976, 23.97 ect). The error scales lineraly with the frame index and eventually runs past the end of the asset, so eventually the next read() returns false.
Here is a short test which demonstrates the issue:
double frame_ms = 1000.0 / cap.get(cv::CAP_PROP_FPS);
for (int N : {24, 50, 100, 200, 400}) {
    cap.set(cv::CAP_PROP_POS_FRAMES, N);
    cap.grab();
    double got = cap.get(cv::CAP_PROP_POS_MSEC);
    printf("N=%d got=%.3f expect=%.3f delta=%+.3f ms\n",
           N, got, N * frame_ms, got - N * frame_ms);
}
Running on a 23.976 fps file:
N=24  got=1043.478   expect=1001.000   delta=+42.478 ms
N=50  got=2173.913   expect=2085.417   delta=+88.496 ms
N=100 got=4347.826   expect=4170.833   delta=+176.993 ms
N=200 got=8695.652   expect=8341.666   delta=+353.986 ms
N=400 got=17391.304  expect=16683.333  delta=+707.971 ms

The drift is 24000/(23*1001) - 1 ≈ 4.244% per frame index. At high indices the seek lands past the end of the file and read() returns false.
I think I can see the cause pretty clearly in modules/videoio/src/cap_avfoundation_mac.mm:
case cv::CAP_PROP_POS_FRAMES:
    retval = mAssetTrack.nominalFrameRate > 0
        ? setupReadingAt(CMTimeMake(value, mAssetTrack.nominalFrameRate))
        : false;
    break;
mAssetTrack.nominalFrameRate is a float, CMTimeMake's second argument is a CMTimeScale i.e a int32_t. The implicit narrowing can cause 23.976 -> 23, so frame N requested at N / 23 s instead of a real PTS  N x 1001/24000 s.
It should be pretty straight forward here to use the tracks native rational frame duration (which is integer/integer straight from container) and multiply it by the frame index.
case cv::CAP_PROP_POS_FRAMES: {
    CMTime frameDuration = mAssetTrack.minFrameDuration;
    if (frameDuration.timescale > 0 && frameDuration.value > 0) {
        retval = setupReadingAt(CMTimeMultiply(frameDuration, (int32_t)value));
    } else if (mAssetTrack.nominalFrameRate > 0) {
        retval = setupReadingAt(CMTimeMake(value, mAssetTrack.nominalFrameRate));
    } else {
        retval = false;
    }
    break;
}
I will apply this fix in PR and include a test case + new test file on the "extra" repo since from what I can see, there is no non-integer rate test cases.
Steps to reproduce
Testing logic shown above. Any non-integer media which is long enough should produce similar results.
