System Information
OpenCV version: 4.x (latest main branch)
Operating System / Platform: Windows 11
Compiler & compiler version: MSVC 19.38
Detailed description
fitEllipseDirect and fitEllipseAMS produce incorrect results (silently fall back to
fitEllipseNoDirect or apply noise perturbation) when given near-circular ellipse data
with more than approximately 100 points.
Common root cause: Both functions normalize the design matrix by DM *= (1.0/n),
which causes det(M) to shrink as a power of n. The hardcoded threshold 1e-10
does not account for this scaling, leading to false singular-matrix detection.
The manifestation differs by algorithm:
Root cause:
In fitEllipseDirect (shapedescr.cpp, line ~720), the design matrix DM is normalized by
DM *= (1.0/n). This causes det(M) to scale as O(1/n^6). For the 3×3 Schur complement
matrix M, the determinant shrinks rapidly and falls below the hardcoded threshold 1e-10
even though the matrix is perfectly well-conditioned. When this happens, the algorithm enters
the noise-perturbation loop and eventually falls back to fitEllipseNoDirect.
In fitEllipseAMS (shapedescr.cpp, line ~559), the same DM *= (1.0/n) normalization causes
det(M) of the 5×5 matrix to scale as O(1/n^5). The fixed threshold 1e-10 similarly
triggers false singular-matrix detection.
Consequence: For near-circular ellipses (where the conic coefficients are close to
degenerate), even moderate point counts (n=200, n=360) cause both algorithms to fail silently
and return results from the fallback fitEllipseNoDirect method instead of computing their
specialized solutions (Direct/AMS).
Steps to reproduce
#include <opencv2/imgproc.hpp>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <cmath>

int main() {
    // Generate 360 points on a near-circular ellipse (a=17, b=16.5)
    std::vector<cv::Point2f> points;
    double cx = 27.0, cy = 27.0, a = 17.0, b = 16.5;
    for (int i = 0; i < 360; i++) {
        double theta = 2.0 * CV_PI * i / 360.0;
        points.push_back(cv::Point2f(
            (float)(cx + a * std::cos(theta)),
            (float)(cy + b * std::sin(theta))));
    }

    cv::RotatedRect direct = cv::fitEllipseDirect(points);
    cv::RotatedRect noDirect = cv::fitEllipse(points);
    cv::RotatedRect ams = cv::fitEllipseAMS(points);

    std::cout << "fitEllipseDirect: center=(" << direct.center.x << ", "
              << direct.center.y << ") size=(" << direct.size.width << ", "
              << direct.size.height << ")" << std::endl;
    std::cout << "fitEllipse:       center=(" << noDirect.center.x << ", "
              << noDirect.center.y << ") size=(" << noDirect.size.width << ", "
              << noDirect.size.height << ")" << std::endl;
    std::cout << "fitEllipseAMS:    center=(" << ams.center.x << ", "
              << ams.center.y << ") size=(" << ams.size.width << ", "
              << ams.size.height << ")" << std::endl;

    // Expected: ~(27, 27), size ~(33, 34)
    // Bug: fitEllipseDirect returns the same result as fitEllipse
    //      (proving it fell back to fitEllipseNoDirect)
    // Bug: fitEllipseAMS returns distorted results due to noise perturbation
    return 0;
}
