Detailed description
cv::moveWindow(name, x, y) on macOS places the window at y + dockHeight pixels from the top of the visible screen area, instead of y pixels. This only occurs when the dock is visible at the bottom of the screen. Hiding the dock eliminates the offset.
The bug is in modules/highgui/src/window_cocoa.mm. Both code paths (before and after first imshow) compute:
y = [[window screen] visibleFrame].size.height - y;
[window setFrameTopLeftPoint:NSMakePoint(x, y)];
setFrameTopLeftPoint operates in full screen coordinates (origin at bottom-left), but visibleFrame.size.height excludes the dock. When the dock is at the bottom, visibleFrame.origin.y equals the dock height, and the conversion is off by that amount.
Steps to reproduce
#include <opencv2/highgui.hpp>

int main() {
    cv::namedWindow("test", cv::WINDOW_AUTOSIZE);
    cv::moveWindow("test", 100, 0); // should be flush below menu bar
    cv::imshow("test", cv::Mat());
    cv::waitKey(0);
}

Ensure the macOS dock is visible at the bottom of the screen
Run the above — window appears ~85px (dock height) below the menu bar
Hide the dock — window now appears correctly at the top

