Detailed description
This issue draft was prepared with assistance from Microsoft Copilot, and the final content has been reviewed and confirmed by a human
Summary
VideoCaptureParameters stores all parameter values internally as integers (key:int, value:int).
However, several videoio backends still use get<double>() and then immediately cast the result back to an integer.
This double-based access is unnecessary, misleading, and may trigger compiler warnings.
Example of problematic pattern (gstreamer):
double value = params.get<double>(CAP_PROP_AUDIO_STREAM);  
audioStream = static_cast<gint>(value);   // immediately cast back to int

Since the underlying storage is already integer, this double → int round-trip has no meaning.
Correct usage:
gint value = params.get<gint>(CAP_PROP_AUDIO_STREAM);
audioStream = value;


Clang 23 warning
When building OpenCV 4.x with Clang 23, the following warning appears:
warning: overlapping comparisons always evaluate to true [-Wtautological-overlap-compare]  
    if (value == -1 || value > -1)

This warning helped identify the root cause:
double-based parameter access is unnecessary and misleading.



      opencv/modules/videoio/src/cap_gstreamer.cpp


        Lines 500 to 511
      in
      aea90a9







           if (params.has(CAP_PROP_AUDIO_STREAM)) 




           { 




               double value = params.get<double>(CAP_PROP_AUDIO_STREAM); 




               if (value == -1 || value > -1) 




                   audioStream = static_cast<gint>(value); 




               else 




               { 




                   CV_LOG_ERROR(NULL, "VIDEOIO/Gstreamer: CAP_PROP_AUDIO_STREAM parameter value is invalid/unsupported: " << value); 




                   return false; 




               } 




           } 




           return true; 






Affected backends
The following backends contain unnecessary get<double>() usage followed by integer casts:
gstreamer
double value = params.get<double>(CAP_PROP_VIDEO_STREAM);  
double value = params.get<double>(CAP_PROP_AUDIO_STREAM);  
gint value   = static_cast<gint>(params.get<double>(CAP_PROP_AUDIO_DATA_DEPTH));  
int value    = static_cast<int>(params.get<double>(CAP_PROP_AUDIO_SAMPLES_PER_SECOND));  
int value    = static_cast<uint32_t>(params.get<double>(CAP_PROP_AUDIO_SYNCHRONIZE));  

These look correct at first glance, but the immediate static_cast(value) shows that the code actually expects an integer.
msmf
double value = params.get<double>(CAP_PROP_VIDEO_STREAM);  
double value = params.get<double>(CAP_PROP_AUDIO_STREAM);  
int value    = static_cast<int>(params.get<double>(CAP_PROP_AUDIO_DATA_DEPTH));  
int value    = static_cast<int>(params.get<double>(CAP_PROP_AUDIO_SAMPLES_PER_SECOND));  
int value    = static_cast<UINT32>(params.get<double>(CAP_PROP_AUDIO_SYNCHRONIZE));  

obsensor / liborbbec
int color_width  = params.get<double>(CAP_PROP_FRAME_WIDTH);  
int color_height = params.get<double>(CAP_PROP_FRAME_HEIGHT);  
int color_fps    = params.get<double>(CAP_PROP_FPS);  
int depth_width  = params.get<double>(CAP_PROP_OBSENSOR_DEPTH_WIDTH);  
int depth_height = params.get<double>(CAP_PROP_OBSENSOR_DEPTH_HEIGHT);  
int depth_fps    = params.get<double>(CAP_PROP_OBSENSOR_DEPTH_FPS);  

All of these follow the same pattern:
get<double>() is used even though the value is immediately treated as an integer.

Why this matters

Avoids meaningless double → int conversions
Prevents compiler warnings (Clang 23)
Makes backend behavior consistent
Matches the actual design of VideoCaptureParameters
Simplifies code and reduces potential bugs


Proposed plan
I plan to submit PRs backend-by-backend:

gstreamer (first PR)
msmf
obsensor / liborbbec

This keeps each PR small and easy to review.
